1
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:11,222
Last week a release broke the checkout for everyone, and the pipeline was green the whole time.

2
00:00:11,222 --> 00:00:12,980
How does that even happen?

3
00:00:13,533 --> 00:00:16,609
Because the pipeline only asked one question:

4
00:00:16,609 --> 00:00:18,455
did the deployment succeed?

5
00:00:18,455 --> 00:00:23,855
With a normal rolling update, the new version replaces the old pods one by one,

6
00:00:23,855 --> 00:00:27,068
and within minutes it serves every single user.

7
00:00:27,633 --> 00:00:30,230
And then you find out the hard way:

8
00:00:30,230 --> 00:00:32,753
from an alert, or from a customer.

9
00:00:33,300 --> 00:00:39,223
Then a person has to decide, under pressure, whether it is bad enough to roll back.

10
00:00:39,223 --> 00:00:42,434
Usually while the error count keeps climbing.

11
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,054
So what does this project do differently?

12
00:00:46,600 --> 00:00:49,126
Every release goes out as a canary.

13
00:00:49,126 --> 00:00:53,599
The new version first gets only twenty percent of the traffic.

14
00:00:54,133 --> 00:00:59,868
While the traffic grows, the new version is measured against agreed targets,

15
00:00:59,868 --> 00:01:03,263
called SLOs, every fifteen to thirty seconds.

16
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:09,545
If it stays healthy, it is promoted to a hundred percent with nobody in the loop.

17
00:01:09,545 --> 00:01:14,722
If it fails too often, or gets too slow, it is rolled back automatically,

18
00:01:14,722 --> 00:01:17,559
and the old version keeps serving users.

19
00:01:19,300 --> 00:01:21,974
Can you explain it without the jargon?

20
00:01:22,533 --> 00:01:25,459
Think of a restaurant with a new recipe.

21
00:01:25,459 --> 00:01:28,239
You do not serve it to the whole room.

22
00:01:28,239 --> 00:01:31,823
You serve it to one table in five, and you watch.

23
00:01:32,367 --> 00:01:37,056
If those tables are happy, you serve it to two tables in five,

24
00:01:37,600 --> 00:01:39,064
then three,

25
00:01:39,600 --> 00:01:41,049
then four,

26
00:01:41,600 --> 00:01:43,869
and finally to everyone.

27
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:50,814
If the tables complain, you stop at once, and everybody gets the old recipe again.

28
00:01:50,814 --> 00:01:54,083
Only a few tables ever tasted the bad one.

29
00:01:54,633 --> 00:01:56,012
The important part:

30
00:01:56,012 --> 00:02:00,948
how many complaints are too many is agreed before the dinner starts.

31
00:02:00,948 --> 00:02:04,504
Nobody argues about it in the middle of the rush.

32
00:02:04,504 --> 00:02:07,698
In our world, that agreed number is the SLO.

33
00:02:09,433 --> 00:02:13,560
I hear SLO, SLI and error budget all the time.

34
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:15,983
What do they actually mean?

35
00:02:16,533 --> 00:02:21,539
An SLI, a service level indicator, is simply something we measure.

36
00:02:21,539 --> 00:02:22,980
Here there are two:

37
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:29,048
the share of requests that fail with a server error, and how long requests take.

38
00:02:29,048 --> 00:02:30,868
For time we use the p95:

39
00:02:30,868 --> 00:02:35,343
ninety-five percent of requests are faster than this value.

40
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:42,972
The SLO, the service level objective, is the target for that measurement.

41
00:02:42,972 --> 00:02:44,909
For this orders API:

42
00:02:44,909 --> 00:02:51,399
99.5% of requests must succeed, and the p95 must stay under 300 ms.

43
00:02:51,933 --> 00:02:55,292
The error budget is what is left over.

44
00:02:55,292 --> 00:02:59,005
If 99.5% must succeed, then 0.5% may fail.

45
00:02:59,005 --> 00:03:02,011
That is not a failure of the team:

46
00:03:02,011 --> 00:03:09,348
it is a budget the team is allowed to spend, on releases, experiments and bad luck.

47
00:03:09,900 --> 00:03:13,534
And the burn rate says how fast you are spending it.

48
00:03:13,534 --> 00:03:17,868
A burn rate of one spends the budget exactly over thirty days.

49
00:03:17,868 --> 00:03:23,739
A burn rate of 14.4 spends two percent of the whole month's budget in a single hour.

50
00:03:23,739 --> 00:03:26,464
That is fast enough to wake someone up.

51
00:03:28,200 --> 00:03:32,595
So how does the canary check turn the SLO into a yes or no?

52
00:03:33,133 --> 00:03:36,111
With one multiplication.

53
00:03:36,111 --> 00:03:39,337
The error budget is 0.005.

54
00:03:39,337 --> 00:03:44,300
The fastest burn rate we accept is 14.4.

55
00:03:44,300 --> 00:03:47,526
0.005 times 14.4 is 0.072.

56
00:03:47,526 --> 00:03:53,978
So the canary may fail at most 7.2% of its requests.

57
00:03:54,533 --> 00:03:57,728
Below that, the release carries on.

58
00:03:58,267 --> 00:04:04,861
Above it, the canary is burning budget as fast as an incident would, and the release is stopped.

59
00:04:05,400 --> 00:04:07,667
And notice where 14.4 comes from:

60
00:04:07,667 --> 00:04:12,476
it is the same threshold as the alert that pages the on-call engineer.

61
00:04:12,476 --> 00:04:16,048
A release that would page someone is never promoted.

62
00:04:16,048 --> 00:04:20,444
The deployment and the alerting share one definition of healthy.

63
00:04:22,200 --> 00:04:24,333
What is actually running?

64
00:04:24,333 --> 00:04:26,039
Draw me the picture.

65
00:04:26,600 --> 00:04:28,803
It starts in GitHub Actions.

66
00:04:28,803 --> 00:04:34,389
After the tests, a release is just a Helm upgrade with a new image tag.

67
00:04:34,933 --> 00:04:37,015
Argo Rollouts receives it.

68
00:04:37,015 --> 00:04:45,184
It replaces the normal Kubernetes Deployment with a Rollout, which knows how to move traffic in steps:

69
00:04:45,184 --> 00:04:49,028
twenty, forty, sixty, eighty, a hundred percent.

70
00:04:49,567 --> 00:04:52,723
During a release there are two kinds of pods:

71
00:04:52,723 --> 00:04:57,984
the stable pods with the old version, and the canary pods with the new one.

72
00:04:57,984 --> 00:05:01,771
Every pod exposes metrics, labelled with its revision.

73
00:05:02,333 --> 00:05:08,731
In a lab there are no real users, so a small load generator sends twenty requests per second.

74
00:05:08,731 --> 00:05:11,758
Without traffic there is nothing to measure.

75
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:19,054
Prometheus scrapes every pod, computes the SLIs, and evaluates the burn-rate alerts.

76
00:05:19,600 --> 00:05:26,129
Every fifteen to thirty seconds, Argo Rollouts asks Prometheus about the canary pods only.

77
00:05:26,667 --> 00:05:30,469
Grafana shows all of it on one dashboard:

78
00:05:30,469 --> 00:05:35,476
the SLOs, the error budget, and canary against stable.

79
00:05:36,033 --> 00:05:38,575
And the answer decides the release:

80
00:05:38,575 --> 00:05:44,603
pass means promote, fail means abort, and all traffic goes back to the stable pods.

81
00:05:46,367 --> 00:05:49,106
Where are all these numbers written down?

82
00:05:49,667 --> 00:05:50,695
In one place:

83
00:05:50,695 --> 00:05:52,355
the Helm values file.

84
00:05:52,355 --> 00:05:58,286
The SLO targets, the burn rate, how often to measure, and the canary steps.

85
00:05:58,286 --> 00:06:05,561
Changing the policy is a reviewed pull request, not a decision in the middle of an incident.

86
00:06:06,100 --> 00:06:08,371
Two settings deserve attention.

87
00:06:08,371 --> 00:06:16,430
failureLimit 1 means one bad measurement is forgiven, because one noisy sample should not kill a good release.

88
00:06:16,430 --> 00:06:18,334
The second bad one aborts.

89
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:20,805
And consecutiveErrorLimit 4:

90
00:06:20,805 --> 00:06:27,471
if Prometheus cannot answer five times in a row, the analysis gives up and the release is aborted.

91
00:06:27,471 --> 00:06:31,144
We will break Prometheus on purpose later to prove it.

92
00:06:31,700 --> 00:06:38,609
With five replicas, each step of twenty percent moves exactly one pod from the old version to the new.

93
00:06:40,367 --> 00:06:43,561
And what exactly does it ask Prometheus?

94
00:06:44,100 --> 00:06:46,256
This is the AnalysisTemplate.

95
00:06:46,256 --> 00:06:49,526
The first check is the canary's error ratio:

96
00:06:49,526 --> 00:06:54,134
failed requests divided by all requests, over the last minute.

97
00:06:54,700 --> 00:06:57,242
The important detail is the selector.

98
00:06:57,242 --> 00:07:04,251
Every pod carries a label with the hash of its revision, and Argo Rollouts fills in the canary's hash.

99
00:07:04,251 --> 00:07:08,304
So the stable pods cannot hide a bad canary in the average.

100
00:07:08,867 --> 00:07:12,549
The success condition has a second safety detail:

101
00:07:12,549 --> 00:07:14,202
the result must exist.

102
00:07:14,202 --> 00:07:20,515
If no traffic reached the canary, there is no data, and no data counts as a failure.

103
00:07:20,515 --> 00:07:23,671
A version nobody tested is never promoted.

104
00:07:24,233 --> 00:07:32,778
The second check, below it, works the same way for the p95 latency, with the 300 ms limit.

105
00:07:34,533 --> 00:07:36,444
The canary is protected.

106
00:07:36,444 --> 00:07:40,903
What about problems that show up later, after a release?

107
00:07:41,467 --> 00:07:44,414
That is the job of the burn-rate alerts.

108
00:07:44,414 --> 00:07:47,067
They follow the Google SRE Workbook.

109
00:07:47,067 --> 00:07:52,373
The fast-burn alert pages when the budget burns fourteen times too fast,

110
00:07:52,373 --> 00:07:56,131
over one hour, and also over the last five minutes.

111
00:07:56,667 --> 00:07:57,840
Why two windows?

112
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:01,726
The long window proves it is significant, not a blip.

113
00:08:01,726 --> 00:08:08,911
The short window proves it is still happening now, so the alert stops soon after the problem does.

114
00:08:09,467 --> 00:08:17,641
A slower burn over six hours also pages, and a slow leak over a whole day opens a ticket instead of waking anyone up.

115
00:08:18,200 --> 00:08:20,830
There is a matching alert for latency.

116
00:08:20,830 --> 00:08:27,475
And all four rules are unit-tested with promtool, so we know they fire exactly when they should.

117
00:08:29,233 --> 00:08:33,453
Can I see it working for real, not just on slides?

118
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:39,538
Everything you will see now is a real run, recorded on my laptop.

119
00:08:39,538 --> 00:08:43,627
One script, scripts/up.sh, builds the whole lab:

120
00:08:43,627 --> 00:08:48,057
a local Kubernetes cluster with kind, Argo Rollouts,

121
00:08:48,057 --> 00:08:52,998
Prometheus with the SLO rules, Grafana with the dashboard,

122
00:08:52,998 --> 00:08:58,110
and version 1.0.0 of the orders API with its load generator.

123
00:08:58,667 --> 00:09:00,904
It took 2 min 21 s.

124
00:09:00,904 --> 00:09:04,791
No cloud account, nothing to pay.

125
00:09:05,333 --> 00:09:09,110
The cluster has one control plane and two worker nodes,

126
00:09:09,110 --> 00:09:14,398
so the stable and canary pods spread across machines, like in a real cluster.

127
00:09:16,133 --> 00:09:18,058
What am I looking at?

128
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:22,210
This is the Argo Rollouts view of the service.

129
00:09:22,210 --> 00:09:26,604
Status healthy, step eight of eight, weight one hundred:

130
00:09:26,604 --> 00:09:31,470
version 1.0.0 is the stable version, and it serves everything.

131
00:09:32,033 --> 00:09:33,747
Below it, the tree:

132
00:09:33,747 --> 00:09:37,804
one ReplicaSet, revision one, with five pods.

133
00:09:37,804 --> 00:09:39,608
Remember this shape.

134
00:09:39,608 --> 00:09:44,748
During a release, a second ReplicaSet appears next to it.

135
00:09:46,500 --> 00:09:48,189
And the dashboard?

136
00:09:48,733 --> 00:09:49,602
At the top:

137
00:09:49,602 --> 00:09:54,498
availability one hundred percent, the whole error budget left,

138
00:09:54,498 --> 00:09:59,473
burn rate zero, latency inside the target, and no alert firing.

139
00:10:00,033 --> 00:10:00,475
Below:

140
00:10:00,475 --> 00:10:07,759
the burn rate over several windows, and then the error ratio and the p95 latency split by revision.

141
00:10:07,759 --> 00:10:13,793
That split is what lets us compare a canary with the stable version, side by side.

142
00:10:15,533 --> 00:10:18,096
Now release a new version.

143
00:10:18,096 --> 00:10:19,378
What happens?

144
00:10:19,933 --> 00:10:25,636
The test script, e2e.sh, releases version 1.1.0, a healthy version.

145
00:10:25,636 --> 00:10:31,764
It uses shorter pauses than production, thirty seconds instead of sixty,

146
00:10:31,764 --> 00:10:35,253
so the whole proof fits in a few minutes.

147
00:10:35,253 --> 00:10:38,658
The decision rules are exactly the same.

148
00:10:39,200 --> 00:10:40,883
Look at the tree now.

149
00:10:40,883 --> 00:10:48,016
Revision two appeared, with one canary pod, next to the four stable pods of revision one.

150
00:10:48,016 --> 00:10:49,138
Weight twenty:

151
00:10:49,138 --> 00:10:50,420
one pod in five.

152
00:10:50,420 --> 00:10:55,789
And there is an AnalysisRun, the background check, already running.

153
00:10:57,533 --> 00:11:00,516
Argo Rollouts also has a dashboard.

154
00:11:00,516 --> 00:11:03,670
Here the release is at sixty percent:

155
00:11:03,670 --> 00:11:08,783
the steps on the left, three canary pods with version 1.1.0,

156
00:11:08,783 --> 00:11:14,238
two stable pods with 1.0.0, and the analysis run doing its work.

157
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:17,399
So nobody pressed a button here?

158
00:11:17,933 --> 00:11:18,523
Nobody.

159
00:11:18,523 --> 00:11:25,513
Every step waits, the analysis keeps measuring, and the rollout moves on by itself.

160
00:11:27,267 --> 00:11:34,456
After 2 min 33 s, version 1.1.0 was promoted to a hundred percent.

161
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:38,155
These are the real measurements.

162
00:11:38,155 --> 00:11:42,098
9 error-ratio checks, the highest 0.000.

163
00:11:42,098 --> 00:11:51,069
9 latency checks, with a p95 of about 24 milliseconds, far under the 300 millisecond limit.

164
00:11:51,633 --> 00:11:55,206
A boring release, exactly as it should be.

165
00:11:55,206 --> 00:11:57,503
And nobody had to watch it.

166
00:11:59,267 --> 00:12:01,146
And a bad release?

167
00:12:01,700 --> 00:12:04,220
Version 1.2.0 is broken on purpose.

168
00:12:04,220 --> 00:12:08,972
The fault injection makes it fail twenty-five percent of requests:

169
00:12:08,972 --> 00:12:15,019
the kind of bug that slips through tests because it only shows up with real traffic.

170
00:12:15,567 --> 00:12:19,162
And here is the rollout a few seconds later.

171
00:12:19,162 --> 00:12:19,734
Status:

172
00:12:19,734 --> 00:12:20,470
degraded.

173
00:12:20,470 --> 00:12:21,777
Rollout aborted.

174
00:12:21,777 --> 00:12:23,902
The message even says why:

175
00:12:23,902 --> 00:12:30,440
the metric canary-error-ratio failed two times, and only one failure is allowed.

176
00:12:30,440 --> 00:12:32,646
The weight is back to zero.

177
00:12:34,400 --> 00:12:39,660
On Grafana you can see the spike, and you can see whose spike it is:

178
00:12:39,660 --> 00:12:43,064
the error ratio of the canary revision only.

179
00:12:43,064 --> 00:12:45,695
The stable revision stays at zero.

180
00:12:46,233 --> 00:12:50,399
That is why the analysis looks at the canary's own pods.

181
00:12:50,399 --> 00:12:54,714
Mixed into the average, one pod's errors would look small.

182
00:12:54,714 --> 00:12:57,988
Measured alone, they are impossible to miss.

183
00:12:59,733 --> 00:13:04,040
The two measurements were 0.305 and 0.244:

184
00:13:04,040 --> 00:13:08,346
about the twenty-five percent we injected.

185
00:13:08,346 --> 00:13:10,294
The limit is 0.072.

186
00:13:10,294 --> 00:13:14,498
Two failures, so the rollout was aborted.

187
00:13:15,033 --> 00:13:16,729
From release to abort:

188
00:13:16,729 --> 00:13:17,577
42 seconds.

189
00:13:17,577 --> 00:13:25,903
Then the test sent forty requests through the Service and checked that every answer came from version 1.1.0.

190
00:13:25,903 --> 00:13:28,678
Users were back on the good version.

191
00:13:29,233 --> 00:13:34,613
So only one pod in five ever served the bad version, and for less than a minute.

192
00:13:35,167 --> 00:13:35,858
Exactly.

193
00:13:35,858 --> 00:13:40,701
With a rolling update, it would have reached every user.

194
00:13:42,467 --> 00:13:46,601
What if the new version does not fail, but is just slow?

195
00:13:47,167 --> 00:13:49,042
That is release three.

196
00:13:49,042 --> 00:13:55,520
Version 1.3.0 answers correctly, but adds 600 milliseconds to every request.

197
00:13:55,520 --> 00:14:01,231
No errors at all, so an error check alone would happily promote it.

198
00:14:01,767 --> 00:14:03,918
The latency check caught it:

199
00:14:03,918 --> 00:14:10,831
canary-p95-latency failed twice, and the rollout was aborted 43 seconds after the release.

200
00:14:11,367 --> 00:14:14,447
The measured p95 was 0.975 seconds.

201
00:14:14,447 --> 00:14:16,207
Why not exactly 0.6?

202
00:14:16,207 --> 00:14:22,368
Because the histogram's buckets jump from half a second to one second,

203
00:14:22,368 --> 00:14:26,152
and Prometheus estimates inside the bucket.

204
00:14:26,152 --> 00:14:30,465
The estimate is rough, but the breach is obvious:

205
00:14:30,465 --> 00:14:32,841
far above 300 milliseconds.

206
00:14:33,400 --> 00:14:34,811
And the last line:

207
00:14:34,811 --> 00:14:37,319
all end-to-end scenarios passed.

208
00:14:37,319 --> 00:14:42,804
One good release promoted, two bad releases rolled back by themselves.

209
00:14:44,567 --> 00:14:46,242
Here it is on Grafana:

210
00:14:46,242 --> 00:14:50,431
the p95 of the canary revision jumps to about a second,

211
00:14:50,431 --> 00:14:55,076
while the stable revision stays at about twenty milliseconds.

212
00:14:56,833 --> 00:15:01,142
What if Prometheus itself goes down in the middle of a release?

213
00:15:01,142 --> 00:15:03,673
Does the release just continue blind?

214
00:15:04,233 --> 00:15:07,164
That is exactly what this test checks.

215
00:15:07,164 --> 00:15:11,328
It releases version 1.4.0, which is perfectly healthy,

216
00:15:11,328 --> 00:15:16,418
and while the canary is running it scales Prometheus down to zero.

217
00:15:16,967 --> 00:15:20,211
The analysis can no longer measure anything.

218
00:15:20,211 --> 00:15:26,626
After five errors in a row, the analysis run ends in Error, and the rollout is aborted.

219
00:15:27,167 --> 00:15:30,157
Even though 1.4.0 was actually fine!

220
00:15:30,157 --> 00:15:31,735
That is on purpose:

221
00:15:31,735 --> 00:15:35,888
a release that cannot be verified is not promoted.

222
00:15:35,888 --> 00:15:37,466
Failing safe means:

223
00:15:37,466 --> 00:15:41,286
when in doubt, keep the version we know works.

224
00:15:41,833 --> 00:15:46,293
Then the script brings Prometheus back and restores the stable version.

225
00:15:48,033 --> 00:15:50,533
Is the service itself secure?

226
00:15:51,067 --> 00:15:56,682
A NetworkPolicy lets traffic in only from the same namespace and from monitoring.

227
00:15:56,682 --> 00:15:58,623
The security test proves it:

228
00:15:58,623 --> 00:16:04,376
a pod in the same namespace reaches the API, a pod in another namespace is blocked.

229
00:16:04,933 --> 00:16:09,928
The containers run as a non-root user, with a read-only file system,

230
00:16:09,928 --> 00:16:13,968
no Linux capabilities, and the default seccomp profile.

231
00:16:14,533 --> 00:16:19,641
The pods get no Kubernetes API token, because they do not need one.

232
00:16:19,641 --> 00:16:24,901
And the Grafana admin password is generated when the lab is deployed,

233
00:16:24,901 --> 00:16:28,103
so nothing secret lives in the repository.

234
00:16:28,667 --> 00:16:36,081
In CI, the image is scanned with Trivy, and the build fails on any fixable high or critical vulnerability.

235
00:16:37,833 --> 00:16:42,063
How do you know the safety net itself still works next month?

236
00:16:42,600 --> 00:16:45,739
Because it is tested on every change.

237
00:16:45,739 --> 00:16:48,029
The service has unit tests.

238
00:16:48,567 --> 00:16:51,736
The SLO rules are checked with promtool,

239
00:16:52,300 --> 00:16:54,277
and the alerts are unit-tested:

240
00:16:54,277 --> 00:17:00,143
promtool feeds them made-up metrics and checks that each alert fires exactly when it should,

241
00:17:00,143 --> 00:17:02,119
and stays quiet when it should.

242
00:17:02,667 --> 00:17:05,283
GitHub Actions runs three stages:

243
00:17:05,283 --> 00:17:08,851
static checks, then the image build and scan,

244
00:17:09,400 --> 00:17:13,352
and then the full end-to-end run on a fresh kind cluster:

245
00:17:13,352 --> 00:17:19,592
the good release, the two bad releases, the monitoring outage and the NetworkPolicy check.

246
00:17:19,592 --> 00:17:22,919
If the safety net breaks, the pipeline goes red.

247
00:17:24,667 --> 00:17:26,636
Let me try to sum it up.

248
00:17:26,636 --> 00:17:29,181
First, measure what users feel:

249
00:17:29,181 --> 00:17:33,366
errors and latency, separately for the new version.

250
00:17:33,900 --> 00:17:39,154
Second, agree on the limits before the release, not during the incident.

251
00:17:39,700 --> 00:17:44,109
Third, give the new version only a part of the traffic first.

252
00:17:44,667 --> 00:17:48,085
Fourth, let the numbers decide.

253
00:17:48,085 --> 00:17:49,629
Pass, promote.

254
00:17:49,629 --> 00:17:51,393
Fail, roll back.

255
00:17:51,393 --> 00:17:54,591
And no data counts as a fail.

256
00:17:55,133 --> 00:17:55,870
And fifth:

257
00:17:55,870 --> 00:18:00,876
test the safety net itself, with real bad releases, on every change.

258
00:18:00,876 --> 00:18:02,790
That is the whole project.

259
00:18:02,790 --> 00:18:06,692
The repository has a beginner study guide with a PDF,

260
00:18:06,692 --> 00:18:14,643
if you want to learn every tool from zero, and you can run all of this on your own laptop with two commands.
